  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nWith the large stacked low responsible for all the rainfall now\nspread from the eastern Great Lakes into eastern Canada, cooler\nand drier central Canadian high pressure is making inroads into\nthe north-central CONUS. Low clouds are still lingering around \nthe western periphery of the deep low along with a trailing weak\nlow-level trough in the wake of this system. The cloud cover \nwill remain over the southern half of MN and most of WI through \ntonight and the first half of tomorrow, finally breaking up a \nbit tomorrow afternoon, then the clouds are expected to return \nFriday night into Saturday morning. These clouds will actually \nhave a fairly significant impact on temperatures, shrinking down\nthe diurnal range more than in previous forecasts. Highs both \ntoday and Friday will remain in the 40s, while lows will range \nfrom the upper 20s to lower 30s both tonight and Friday night. \n(The more significant impact are those lows Friday night into \nSaturday morning which previously looked to reach hard freeze \nterritory, but now appear to stay warmer than previous \nforecasts.) As this high pressure airmass moves slowly southeast\nacross the region, the low thick low level moisture will not \nhave much of an opportunity to be scoured out, at least not \nuntil Sunday when a dry cold front swings through the region, \npushing the moisture finally out of the Upper Midwest.\n\nWe will see an increase in temperatures Sunday-Monday with more \nmixing which will make for a decrease in cloud cover starting \nSunday, followed by brief warm-sectoring on Monday ahead of \nanother dry cold front expected to push through late Monday. \nHighs on Sunday will creep into the mid-upper 50s followed by \nhighs peaking in the upper 50s to lower 60s. Behind that Monday \ncold front, high temperatures will fall back down to range from \nthe upper 40s to mid 50s with lows mainly in the 30s but with no\nhard freezes expected.\n\nAs for precipitation, none can be expected in the WFO MPX\ncoverage area through at least the next week and potentially \nthrough the middle of November. So this will allow the soaking \nrains received over the past few days to infiltrate the ground, \nelevate lakes and rivers and put some dents into the prolonged \ndrought conditions across the Upper Midwest.",
